# npm refuses to build the dependency tree for ESLint 10 because three plugins
# that eslint-config-next pulls in still declare a peer range that stops at
# ESLint 9. None of them has a release that declares ESLint 10, and none
# publishes a prerelease tag that does, so there is no version to bump to:
#
#   eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y@6.10.2   peer eslint "^3 || ... || ^9"   (last release 2024-10-26)
#     support issue #1075 open, PR #1081 open, unreleased
#   eslint-plugin-react@7.37.5      peer eslint "^3 || ... || ^9.7" (last release 2025-04-03)
#     support issue #4027 open, PR #4022 open, unreleased
#   eslint-plugin-import@2.32.0     peer eslint "^2 || ... || ^9"   (last release 2025-06-20)
#     support issue #3227 closed, PR #3230 merged 2026-10-12 but no npm release yet
#
# They do run correctly on ESLint 10 at runtime: `eslint .` on 10.15.0 reports
# exactly the same 201 files / 70 warnings / 0 errors as on 9.39.5, with the
# same rule IDs, so every rule this config enables loads and runs. ESLint 10
# only removed APIs that were already gone in v9, and these plugins are
# v9-ready. (eslint-plugin-import's known ESLint 10 crash is in
# import/no-default-export, which this config does not enable.)
#
# Remove this as soon as all three ship ESLint 10 support: it switches off
# npm's peer-dependency validation for the whole tree, which is a real safety
# net. The moment it is gone, `npm install` will fail again until the plugins
# catch up, which is the intended signal.
legacy-peer-deps=true
